---
// "One currency, three fidelities": tier cards + the where-you-start callout.
---

<section id="tiers" class="cql-tiers">
  <div class="cql-container cql-section">
    <h2 class="cql-h2">Three fidelities. Match the tier to your API.</h2>
    <p class="cql-lead">
      Every price is in <strong>work-time (ms)</strong>: how much real work a query causes
      on your server. The tiers are how much of that work costQL can <em>see</em>. No tier
      is better than another: the right one is a fact about your API and what it does.
    </p>

    <div class="cql-tiers__grid">
      <div class="cql-tier">
        <span class="cql-tier__tab">T1</span>
        <h3 class="cql-tier__heading">Works with any API</h3>
        <div class="cql-tier__body">
          costQL times whole queries from the outside. No server changes at all. Every API
          starts here — and for an API that does its work one step at a time, this is
          also the fit, not just the first step.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cql-tier">
        <span class="cql-tier__tab">T2</span>
        <h3 class="cql-tier__heading">Sees parallel work</h3>
        <div class="cql-tier__body">
          Your server times each resolver as it runs, so work that happens in parallel stops
          hiding inside one stopwatch. Prices track the real work, not just elapsed time.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cql-tier">
        <span class="cql-tier__tab">T3</span>
        <h3 class="cql-tier__heading">Sees shared work</h3>
        <div class="cql-tier__body">
          Your server reports when it batches or reuses work, and costQL prices that work
          once instead of many times. The fit for APIs that batch their reads or funnel
          queries onto a few shared entities.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cql-callout cql-callout--panel">
      <span class="cql-callout__label">Where you'll start:</span>
      <span>
        your first pack will be T1. It works today, with no server changes, and it already
        gives you a safe max. The packs in the playground are T3 because we run those demo
        servers ourselves, and they batch their reads. Which tier fits your API — and the
        one server change the instrumented tiers ask for — is in <a href="/docs/tiers/"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">the tiers guide</a>.
      </span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
